"An earlier lesson in this unit described the six excesses as six separate categories, but Chinese medicine recognizes that more than one excess frequently invades together, and one particular combination — damp-heat — is common enough to deserve its own name. Dampness on its own tends to produce heaviness and symptoms slow to resolve; heat on its own tends to produce redness and rapid onset. Combined, they don't simply add together — they interact, the way a lid trapped over a simmering, humid pot produces something different from either steam or heat alone.": "7c2bd6e1d890",
"An earlier lesson in this unit described the six excesses as six separate categories, but Chinese medicine recognizes that more than one excess frequently invades together, and one particular combination — damp-heat — is common enough to deserve its own name. Dampness on its own tends to produce heaviness and symptoms slow to resolve; heat on its own tends to produce redness and rapid onset. Combined, they don't simply add together — they interact, the way a lid trapped over a simmering, humid pot produces something different from either steam or heat alone. This combination is traditionally described using a cooking metaphor: dampness is like water, heat is like fire, and damp-heat is what happens when the two are sealed together in a covered pot — a slow, sticky, hard-to-clear steam rather than a quick boil-off. Symptoms traditionally associated with damp-heat reflect this: skin conditions that are both inflamed and oozing, digestive trouble that is both urgent and sluggish, and a general sensation of being weighed down while also running warm — a combination that neither pure dampness nor pure heat produces on its own. Recognizing damp-heat as its own category, rather than trying to treat it as dampness with a side of heat, matters because clearing heat alone can dry out and worsen dampness, while draining dampness alone can leave heat with nothing to disperse it. This is a small but clear example of why the six excesses were never meant to be diagnosed one at a time in isolation — patterns combine, and the combined pattern often behaves differently from the sum of its parts.": "e7707b3f39f4",
